# GC202: Surgery may cure your cancer, surgical oncology

- **Role of surgery for cancer:**
  - Prevention - e.g. for premalignant conditions
  - Diagnosis
  - Staging
  - Treat
  - Palliation

## How cancer spreads

- Modes of cancer spread:
  - **Local spread** - primary site and adjacent tissues or organs
  - **Regional spread** - LN, perineural
  - **Distant metastasis** - transperitoneum, haematogenous
- Causes of mortality related to cancer:
  - Cancer invasion of organ of origin, adjacent organs or distant site - loss of organ function
  - Cancer cachexia - esophageal, pancreatic, liver cancers most prominent due to compression onto GI tract:
    - Principally due to anorexia –\> weight loss, malnutrition
    - Due to infection

## Principles of cancer surgery

- Excision margins (\> 1cm) - complete extirpation of tumour with a rim of tumor free tissue
- En-bloc resection of primary tumour with adjacent resectable organ or tumour - e.g. Whipple
- Preservation of organ function
- Minimal manipulation of tumour and tumour bearing organs - no touch operations
- LN dissection - clearance of lymphatics drainage (for those with potential with lymp) - staging, and prevent microscopic or macroscopic spread
- Minimal blood loss

## Measurements of surgical oncology outcomes

- Hospital mortality rate
- 30d operative mortality rate
- 5y disease free survival
- 5y overall survival rate
- QoL
- What is cure? - 5y disease free survival (many times, recurrence within 5y)

## Factors contributing to outcomes

- surgical technique blood loss
- Completeness of tumour clearance
- Function of organ remnant
- Close surveillance after surgery after surgery and prompt treat of recurrence
- TMN staging at time of surgery
- Microscopic spread –\> recurrence can occur after curative operative –\> requires adjuvant therapy to eradication or control of microscopic foci (indicated for advanced disease or high risk of recurrence)
- High risk of recurrence:
  - Advanced TNM staging
  - Genetic markers
  - Vascular permeation by cancer cells on histological examination
  - Regional LN involvement on resected specimen
